    {"id": "aa-r9", "node": "q-aa-inversion", "text": "When never, seldom, rarely, scarcely or hardly stands alone at the start of a sentence (with no 'than'/'when' partner later on), the auxiliary verb moves ahead of the subject, exactly as in a question: Never have I seen such courage; Seldom does he visit his village.",
     "ok": "Seldom does he visit his village.", "bad": "Seldom he visits his village."},
    {"id": "aa-r10", "node": "q-aa-enough", "text": "'Enough' is placed right after the adjective or adverb it modifies, never before it, and that adjective or adverb stays in its plain (positive) form, never comparative or superlative: brave enough, fast enough, not 'braver enough' or 'enough brave'.",
     "ok": "She is wise enough to understand the risk.", "bad": "She is enough wise to understand the risk."},
